.TH CHTTPCLIENT_SET_POOL_SIZE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_set_pool_size \- set the connection pool size
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_set_pool_size(chttpcli cli, size_t n);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function sets the maximum number of requests that can be in
flight at the same time. Once the client reaches that limit, a caller
of chttpclient_do blocks until one slot becomes free.

This cap applies only to Tier 1 (chttpclient_do and
chttpclient_do_streaming). It applies neither to Tier 2
(chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_async_streaming) nor to
Tier 3 (chttpclient_do_pooled and chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming):
you can submit any number of those requests at the same time, the
shared async engine handles all of them, and a submit never blocks.

An n of 0 selects the CPU count, which is the default.

You can call this function before or after the client sends its first
request, and it takes effect at once. If the limit grows, the client
broadcasts and wakes every caller that waits. This is only a cap on
concurrency, with no link to the separate keep-alive cache of idle
connections, which has its own fixed internal caps for each origin and
in total.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli   The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
n     The pool size. 0 means the CPU count.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            The client has the new pool size.
ccol_invalid_args       cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale handle
                        of a client that is already destroyed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_create_chttpclient (3),
.BR chttpclient_do (3)
